(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-eye-exam-quiz"]={"title":"Eye Examination — Set 1 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Equipment needed for a comprehensive eye exam includes an eye chart, pupil gauge, pen light, and...","choices":["a stethoscope","a reflex hammer","an ophthalmoscope","a nasal speculum"],"answer":2,"exp":"The listed equipment for a comprehensive eye exam includes an eye chart, pupil gauge, pen light, and an ophthalmoscope for fundoscopic examination. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"The lateral rectus muscle, innervated by cranial nerve VI (abducens), functions to...","choices":["move the eye downward, toward the chin (depression)","move the eye outward, away from the nose (abduction)","move the eye upward, toward the brow (elevation)","move the eye inward, toward the nose (adduction)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lateral rectus, innervated by CN (cranial nerve) VI, abducts the eye (moves it outward, away from the nose) — the classic mnemonic 'LR6' reflects this pairing. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"The superior oblique muscle, innervated by cranial nerve IV (trochlear), functions to...","choices":["have no meaningful role whatsoever in normal extraocular eye movement","rotate the top of the eye toward the nose (intorsion) and also move the eye downward (depression)","move the eye inward, toward the nose (adduction), with no torsional component","move the eye outward, away from the nose (abduction), and rotate it upward"],"answer":1,"exp":"The superior oblique, innervated by CN (cranial nerve) IV, produces intorsion (rotating the top of the eye toward the nose around the long axis) and also contributes to depression of the eye — the classic 'SO4' pairing. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve innervates all extraocular muscles except the lateral rectus (CN (cranial nerve) VI) and superior oblique (CN (cranial nerve) IV)?","choices":["Cranial nerve III (oculomotor)","Cranial nerve II (optic)","Cranial nerve V (trigeminal)","Cranial nerve VII (facial)"],"answer":0,"exp":"CN (cranial nerve) III (oculomotor) innervates the superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, and inferior oblique — all the extraocular muscles except the lateral rectus (CN (cranial nerve) VI, abducens) and superior oblique (CN (cranial nerve) IV, trochlear), following the classic 'SO4 LR6, all the rest CN3' mnemonic. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"Before testing visual acuity with a Snellen wall chart, the patient should be positioned at a distance of...","choices":["50 feet","100 feet","6 inches","20 feet"],"answer":3,"exp":"The standard Snellen wall chart is used at a distance of 20 feet, while the smaller Snellen card is used at 6 feet and a handheld card at 14 inches — always checking the specific chart's printed instructions for its intended distance. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"When testing visual acuity, a handheld eye chart is typically used at a distance of...","choices":["six feet","20 feet","one foot","14 inches"],"answer":3,"exp":"A handheld visual acuity card is typically used at 14 inches, compared with 20 feet for the wall-mounted Snellen chart and 6 feet for the Snellen card, though the printed instructions on any specific chart should always be checked. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"When performing visual acuity testing, the correct technique is to...","choices":["test both eyes together only the entire time, without ever occluding either eye individually","determine and test only the patient's dominant eye, skipping the non-dominant eye entirely","cover one eye and read the smallest line possible, then repeat with the other eye, then repeat with both eyes open","always test acuity without any regard whatsoever to the patient's corrective lenses or contacts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Visual acuity testing covers one eye at a time (reading the smallest legible line), then repeats with the other eye, and finally repeats with both eyes open, checking with and without corrective lenses if applicable. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 17"},{"q":"In the visual acuity notation 20/40, the top number (20) represents the distance from the chart, and the bottom number (40) represents...","choices":["the diameter of the patient's pupil, as measured in millimeters during testing","the total number of letters the patient missed while reading that line","the patient's age in years at the time the acuity test was performed","the distance at which a person with normal vision could read that same line"],"answer":3,"exp":"In 20/40 notation, the top number is the testing distance (20 feet), and the bottom number is the distance at which a person with normal visual acuity could still read that same line (40 feet) — so 20/40 means the patient must be at 20 feet to read what a person with normal vision could read from 40 feet away, indicating below-normal acuity. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"When recording visual acuity results, the examiner should record the smallest line of print where...","choices":["more than half of the letters are correctly identified","fewer than a quarter of letters are correctly identified","every single letter is read with zero errors","the patient guesses randomly with no correct answers required"],"answer":0,"exp":"The recorded visual acuity line is the smallest line where the patient correctly identifies more than half of the letters, not necessarily every single letter perfectly. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"Visual acuity testing should always be performed...","choices":["before any tests that direct light at the eyes","after the ophthalmoscopic exam, and never before it","only if the patient specifically requests it be done","in any order, since sequence does not matter at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Visual acuity should always be tested before any subsequent tests that involve shining light into the eyes (such as pupillary light reaction or ophthalmoscopy), since those tests can transiently affect the patient's vision/comfort. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"Visual field testing by confrontation serves as a...","choices":["test of pupillary reaction to both direct and consensual light","test of hearing function, unrelated to the visual pathway","screening test for lesions in the anterior and posterior visual pathway","definitive diagnostic test that requires no further workup"],"answer":2,"exp":"Confrontation visual field testing is a bedside screening test for lesions along the visual pathway; a definitive diagnosis of a visual field defect requires automated perimetry testing performed by an ophthalmologist. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 19"},{"q":"During the static finger wiggle test for monocular visual fields, the examiner brings a wiggling finger slowly forward from...","choices":["a position equidistant from the examiner and patient's uncovered eyes, into the center of view until seen","a position directly behind the patient's head, so it never enters the visual field at all","a position directly touching the surface of the patient's open eye at the very start","a position directly in front of the patient's nose only, without ever moving laterally into view"],"answer":0,"exp":"The examiner starts with the hand equidistant from both the examiner's and patient's uncovered eyes and slowly brings the wiggling finger forward into the center of the visual field until the patient reports seeing it, testing each quadrant this way. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"During monocular visual field confrontation testing, an important assumption underlying the test is that...","choices":["the examiner has no visual field deficits of their own","the patient has no visual field deficits of their own","no equipment or technique assumptions are made at all","both of the patient's eyes must remain open throughout"],"answer":0,"exp":"Because the examiner's own visual field serves as the comparison standard for detecting the patient's deficits, the test specifically assumes the examiner has normal, intact visual fields. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"Color vision is screened using pseudoisochromatic color plates; if color vision is intact, the patient should be able to...","choices":["see absolutely nothing distinguishable within the color plate","identify the embedded figure, usually a number, within the plate","correctly read a standard line on a Snellen visual acuity eye chart","identify only simple geometric shapes, never any embedded numbers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Pseudoisochromatic plates embed a figure (typically a number) using dots of subtly different colors; a patient with intact color vision can identify the figure, while a patient with a color vision deficiency may not. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"On inspection of the eyelids, the examiner screens for erythema, edema, lesions, and...","choices":["adequate closure","color vision","visual acuity","pupillary reaction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Eyelid inspection specifically includes checking for erythema, edema, lesions, and adequate closure, which is important for assessing corneal protection. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"To inspect the conjunctiva and sclera, the examiner gently pulls the lower lids down and asks the patient to look...","choices":["straight ahead only, with eyes closed","up, and then left and right","backward, which is not anatomically possible","down only, with no other direction needed"],"answer":1,"exp":"To fully inspect the conjunctiva and sclera, the examiner gently pulls the lower lids down while the patient looks up, then has the patient look left and right, spreading the lids with thumb and index finger for full view if necessary. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"When inspecting the cornea, the examiner uses oblique lighting specifically to note...","choices":["any opacities","visual acuity","color vision","pupil size only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Oblique (angled) lighting on the cornea helps the examiner detect subtle opacities that might not be visible with direct frontal lighting. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"Pupil size should be measured in dim light; a pupil is considered small if it is under approximately...","choices":["10 mm","0.5 mm","there is no defined threshold for a small pupil","3 mm"],"answer":3,"exp":"A pupil measured in dim light is considered small if it is under approximately 3 mm, and large if it exceeds approximately 5 mm. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"A pupil is considered large if it measures greater than approximately...","choices":["there is no defined threshold for a large pupil","20 mm","1 mm","5 mm"],"answer":3,"exp":"A pupil measured in dim light is considered large if it exceeds approximately 5 mm, with under approximately 3 mm being the small-pupil threshold. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"When testing pupillary reaction to light, shining a bright light obliquely into one eye and observing constriction in that same eye is called the...","choices":["near reaction","direct reaction","consensual reaction","corneal light reflection"],"answer":1,"exp":"The direct pupillary reaction is constriction observed in the same eye that is being directly illuminated, distinct from the consensual reaction, which is constriction observed in the opposite, non-illuminated eye. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"The consensual pupillary reaction refers to...","choices":["dilation observed in the opposite eye when light is shone into one eye","constriction observed in the same eye that is being directly illuminated","a pupillary test entirely unrelated to any light exposure or stimulus","constriction observed in the opposite eye when light is shone into one eye"],"answer":3,"exp":"The consensual reaction is pupillary constriction observed in the eye opposite to the one being directly illuminated, reflecting the bilateral neural pathway of the pupillary light reflex; this is best elicited with a hand placed across the bridge of the nose to prevent light from directly reaching the opposite eye. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"Testing near reaction, which assesses pupillary constriction with near effort and convergence, is performed specifically...","choices":["if the reaction to light is impaired or questionable","only in patients under age 10","only using the ophthalmoscope","as the very first pupillary test, before light reaction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Near reaction testing is performed specifically when the pupillary light reaction is impaired or gives a questionable result, helping further characterize the pupillary response. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"During near reaction testing, the examiner holds a finger approximately how far from the patient's eye, having them alternate looking at the finger and into the distance?","choices":["there is no specific distance used","1 meter","10 cm","50 cm"],"answer":2,"exp":"The examiner holds a finger approximately 10 cm from the patient's eye during near reaction testing, asking the patient to alternate looking at the finger and looking into the distance past it; the pupils should constrict with near effort and convergence. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"To assess ocular alignment using corneal light reflection, the examiner stands approximately how far in front of the patient?","choices":["2 feet","6 inches","10 feet","there is no specific distance for this technique"],"answer":0,"exp":"The examiner stands approximately 2 feet in front of the patient, shines a light, and has the patient look at it; in normal alignment, the corneal light reflection should appear slightly medial of the pupil center in each eye. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"In normal ocular alignment, the corneal light reflection should appear...","choices":["far lateral to the pupil, near the outer canthus","slightly medial of the pupil center","directly over the iris periphery, never near the pupil","absent entirely in normal eyes"],"answer":1,"exp":"With normal ocular alignment, the light reflection on the cornea should appear slightly medial to the center of the pupil in both eyes; asymmetry between the eyes suggests ocular misalignment (strabismus). — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"When assessing extraocular movements in the six cardinal directions of gaze using the 'H pattern' technique, the examiner should pause briefly at the far right, up, and down points specifically to detect...","choices":["visual acuity deficits","pupil size","color vision deficits","nystagmus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Briefly pausing at the extreme positions of gaze (far right, up, down, and similarly on the left) allows the examiner to observe for nystagmus (rhythmic involuntary eye oscillation), which may only become apparent at the extremes of gaze. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"During the H-pattern extraocular movement test, the examiner should hold the patient's head still and instructs them to...","choices":["follow the examiner's finger through the six cardinal positions of gaze, without pausing in the middle","look straight ahead the entire time without ever moving their eyes to track anything","keep their eyes closed completely throughout the entire six-position cardinal gaze test","move their head to actively track the finger, rather than moving just their eyes alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"The patient follows the examiner's finger through the six cardinal gaze positions in an H pattern while keeping the head still; the examiner specifically does not pause in the middle transition points, only pausing briefly at the far right/up/down and far left/up/down extremes to check for nystagmus. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"During extraocular movement testing, the examiner observes for any deviation of gaze, nystagmus, and...","choices":["hearing thresholds","lid lag","pupil color","visual acuity numbers"],"answer":1,"exp":"In addition to gaze deviation and nystagmus, the examiner watches for lid lag (a delay of the upper eyelid following the eye's movement) during extraocular movement testing. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"During convergence testing, as the examiner's finger moves inward toward the bridge of the nose, the eyes should normally follow the finger until it is within approximately...","choices":["0 cm (touching the nose) in every normal patient","5 to 8 cm of the nose","50 to 80 cm of the nose","there is no normal range for convergence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Normal convergence allows the eyes to track the approaching finger until it comes within approximately 5-8 cm of the nose before convergence typically breaks down. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 30"},{"q":"When using the ophthalmoscope, the correct hand/eye pairing is to...","choices":["always use the left hand and left eye, regardless of which of the patient's eyes is being examined at that moment","use whichever hand and eye combination feels most comfortable, since it makes no clinical difference at all","hold the scope in the right hand and use the right eye to examine the patient's right eye (and left hand/left eye for the patient's left eye)","always use the right hand and right eye, regardless of which of the patient's eyes is currently being examined"],"answer":2,"exp":"Proper ophthalmoscope technique matches the examiner's hand and eye to the same side as the patient's eye being examined — right hand/right eye for the patient's right eye, left hand/left eye for the patient's left eye — which avoids bumping noses and allows a natural viewing angle. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 32"}]};
